\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \{\uc0\u8232 "question_id": "Q04",\u8232 "marks": [\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 1,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "facts may give rise to a civil claim and also amount to an offence. Merely because a civil claim is maintainable does not mean that the criminal complaint cannot be maintained.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that a dispute can have both civil and criminal aspects despite its civil nature.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 2,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "The Investigating Officer, however, upon investigation of the matter came to the conclusion that the dispute between the parties was a civil dispute.",\u8232 "reason": "It describes a property dispute involving allegedly forged documents but only reports the investigator's characterization as civil.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 3,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "In view of the preponderance of authorities to the contrary, we are satisfied that the High Court was not justified in quashing the proceedings initiated by the appellant against the respondent.",\u8232 "reason": "It rejects the proposition that pending civil scrutiny of document validity prevents criminal proceedings alleging forgery.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 4,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The allegation that forged and fabricated documents are used by the appellant can also be dealt with in the said suit.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly addresses ownership-related civil litigation and explains that alleged forged documents can be dealt with in the civil suit.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 5,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "the dispute was of a civil nature and with an oblique motive it was given a colour of criminal litigation.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly identifies the claimed criminal colouring of a civil dispute but merely reports the respondents' case without stating the court's holding.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 6,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The proceedings could not be quashed only because the respondents had filed a civil suit with respect to the aforesaid documents.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly holds that a civil suit concerning allegedly forged documents does not by itself bar criminal proceedings.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 7,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The proceedings could not be quashed only because the respondents had filed a civil suit with respect to the aforesaid documents.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that pending civil proceedings concerning allegedly forged documents do not alone justify quashing criminal proceedings.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 8,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "nor the dispute being of civil nature was a bar to criminal proceedings, if a case was made out.",\u8232 "reason": "It expressly states that the civil nature of a dispute does not bar criminal proceedings where an offence is made out.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 9,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "facts may give rise to a civil claim and also amount to an offence.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states the governing principle that civil and criminal consequences can coexist.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 10,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "Obtaining signature of a person on blank sheet of papers by itself is not an offence of forgery or the like.",\u8232 "reason": "It discusses when conduct becomes forgery and a bona fide civil dispute, but does not directly address ownership disputes being given criminal colour.",\u8232 "confidence": "medium"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 11,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The purely civil dispute, is sought to be given a colour of a criminal offence to wreak vengeance against the Appellant.",\u8232 "reason": "It expressly recognizes the possibility of giving a purely civil dispute a criminal colour, while finding that conduct abusive here.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 12,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "when the question regarding validity of a document is subjudice in the civil courts, criminal prosecution, on the allegation of the document being forged, cannot be instituted.",\u8232 "reason": "Although quoting the rejected High Court view, the passage immediately states that authorities were to the contrary and the quashing was unjustified.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 13,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "several disputes of a civil nature may also contain the ingredients of criminal offences and if so, will have to be tried as criminal offences",\u8232 "reason": "It directly gives the rule that a civil dispute may also constitute a criminal offence when its ingredients are present.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 14,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "execution of such document (purporting to convey some property of which he is not the owner) is not execution of a false document as defined under section 464 of the Code.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly addresses property ownership and clarifies when an ownership-based sale deed does not constitute a false document or forgery.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 15,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The proceedings could not be quashed only because the respondents had filed a civil suit with respect to the aforesaid documents.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly holds that civil litigation over documents does not alone prevent criminal proceedings alleging forgery.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \}\u8232 ],\u8232 "good_answer_present": "Yes",\u8232 "best_passage_no": 14,\u8232 "caveats": ""\u8232 \}\
